is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispa, ispb
diagnosis_text:
The issue is not congestion on your “internet box”; the internal LAN and the WAN links are healthy (pc1 has 1–2 ms RTT to internal hosts; r1/csw1/csw2 show no queueing or drops). The affected component is the upstream ISP routing policy: ISP-A (ispa) has a default route pointing back to our customer edge (r1), while r1’s default points to ispa. This creates a routing loop for most public internet destinations.

Evidence:
- On r1: default via 203.0.113.1 (ispa). On ispa: default via 203.0.113.2 (r1). This mutual default sends unknown internet traffic back and forth until TTL expires.
- Traceroute from r1 to 1.1.1.1/8.8.8.8 stops at hop 203.0.113.1 (ispa) and then times out; pings from srv1 to 8.8.8.8/198.41.0.4 return “Time to live exceeded,” consistent with a loop.
- Some specific public IPs (e.g., 216.239.35.4) do work because they are explicitly routed between ispa/ispb, so occasional lookups/pages “catch up,” but most new DNS/HTTP destinations stall or time out, matching the “treacle slow” symptom.

Localization: misconfigured default route on ispa (eth0 toward r1) and also on ispb (default via r1). Root cause: ISP edge routers’ default routes incorrectly point to the customer router instead of to their ISP interconnect.

Corrective fix:
- On ispa: change default route to via ispb over the inter-ISP link (e.g., default via 192.0.2.2 dev eth1) or to a proper upstream.
- On ispb: change default to via ispa (192.0.2.1) or proper upstream.
As a temporary mitigation, configure srv1 (10.5.0.4) to forward DNS to a reachable resolver (e.g., 216.239.35.4) to avoid recursive timeouts.
